1
00:00:01,010 --> 00:00:08,290
This learning module will be part one of a two-part series covering approach to interpretation of a head CT.

2
00:00:10,190 --> 00:00:17,410
Following completion of both modules, the learner should be able to evaluate a head CT using a systematic approach

3
00:00:17,410 --> 00:00:22,790
and be able to state the five key features to assess when interpreting a head CT.

4
00:00:23,450 --> 00:00:27,410
In part one, we're going to concentrate on assessment of ventricular enlargement,

5
00:00:27,410 --> 00:00:32,290
categories of hydrocephalus, acute intracranial hemorrhage,

6
00:00:33,730 --> 00:00:40,290
including localization of the hemorrhage as either intraaxial or extraaxial in location.

7
00:00:42,590 --> 00:00:49,850
As we've talked about in other modules, it is important to have a systematic approach when interpreting an imaging examination.

8
00:00:50,730 --> 00:00:55,370
We're going to go through one such search pattern for non-contrast head CT.

9
00:00:55,370 --> 00:01:01,310
First, we're going to begin with an overview, looking at the ventricles and extraaxial spaces,

10
00:01:01,630 --> 00:01:03,910
getting an idea of brain volume.

11
00:01:04,930 --> 00:01:09,710
Then we're going to search specifically for foci of acute intracranial hemorrhage.

12
00:01:10,730 --> 00:01:13,350
During this search, we're going to concentrate on the parenchyma,

13
00:01:13,610 --> 00:01:18,670
and in addition to looking for hemorrhage, we're going to be looking for gray-white matter differentiation,

14
00:01:19,110 --> 00:01:22,430
which will be important when we're assessing for stroke and ischemia.

15
00:01:22,430 --> 00:01:27,470
Then we're going to be looking for underlying parenchymal masses or mass effect.

16
00:01:28,790 --> 00:01:32,050
For completeness, we need to look at everything on the study,

17
00:01:32,170 --> 00:01:39,530
so we're going to then look at bones, the sinus and mastoid air cells to make sure these structures are well aerated,

18
00:01:39,990 --> 00:01:47,670
and then finally the orbits and surrounding soft tissues of the scalp and the image portions of the suprahyoid neck.

19
00:01:47,670 --> 00:01:52,030
Before getting into the details of the search pattern,

20
00:01:52,530 --> 00:01:56,170
let's take a minute to review the concept of windowing as it relates to CT.

21
00:01:56,990 --> 00:02:01,830
Remember that CT images are composed of 3D pixels, termed voxels.

22
00:02:02,810 --> 00:02:05,530
These voxels are displayed as shades of gray,

23
00:02:06,430 --> 00:02:09,550
depending on the attenuation of the tissues that they contain.

24
00:02:10,330 --> 00:02:13,090
High attenuation materials are shaded white,

25
00:02:13,090 --> 00:02:16,150
whereas low attenuation materials are shaded black.

26
00:02:17,750 --> 00:02:21,270
Windowing then sets how these shades of gray are distributed,

27
00:02:21,890 --> 00:02:24,750
which will determine how the image appears to us visually.

28
00:02:25,410 --> 00:02:29,630
The goal then for windowing is to highlight the tissue of interest.

29
00:02:30,310 --> 00:02:31,470
Let's look at some examples.

30
00:02:31,470 --> 00:02:40,390
The default window for head CT is typically termed the brain window,

31
00:02:40,810 --> 00:02:43,310
which gives a nice overview of the brain parenchyma.

32
00:02:44,590 --> 00:02:51,190
We can window the head CT such that the gray-white matter distinction is highlighted.

33
00:02:51,490 --> 00:02:53,590
This is the so-called stroke window,

34
00:02:53,950 --> 00:02:57,410
and you can see with this window as compared to the brain window,

35
00:02:57,410 --> 00:03:03,630
you're able to appreciate the subtle differences in attenuation between the gray matter and the white matter.

36
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:07,880
A blood window, on the other hand,

37
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:12,700
is designed to really bring out the high attenuation hemorrhage,

38
00:03:13,240 --> 00:03:16,920
especially along the surface of the brain adjacent to the calvarium.

39
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:20,960
And then finally, as you can see with the bone window,

40
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:22,580
compared to the other windows,

41
00:03:22,760 --> 00:03:25,780
you can actually see the detail of the bone.

42
00:03:25,780 --> 00:03:29,880
So in this case, you can actually see the difference between the cortical bone,

43
00:03:30,080 --> 00:03:33,060
the inner and outer cortex of the calvarium,

44
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:35,760
versus the medullary or diploic space.

45
00:03:38,170 --> 00:03:40,590
So as we go through our search pattern,

46
00:03:40,770 --> 00:03:44,270
it will be important to remember that we're going to be using all of these different windows.

47
00:03:44,470 --> 00:03:47,670
And we're going to choose the window that's going to highlight the tissue

48
00:03:47,670 --> 00:03:49,230
that we're interested in evaluating.

49
00:03:49,890 --> 00:03:52,830
So we're going to do a general overview in the brain window

50
00:03:52,830 --> 00:03:56,330
when we're specifically looking for gray-white matter differentiation

51
00:03:56,330 --> 00:03:58,510
or subtle parenchymal lesions.

52
00:03:58,750 --> 00:04:00,590
We may look at the stroke window,

53
00:04:01,070 --> 00:04:03,070
looking for high attenuation hemorrhage,

54
00:04:03,170 --> 00:04:05,370
particularly along the surface of the brain,

55
00:04:05,450 --> 00:04:06,830
between the brain and the calvarium.

56
00:04:07,210 --> 00:04:08,490
Blood windows may be helpful.

57
00:04:09,150 --> 00:04:12,170
An assessment of the bones and paranasal sinuses

58
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:15,570
will be best done with bone windows.

59
00:04:15,570 --> 00:04:23,420
This is an example of a structured report for a normal non-contrast head CT.

60
00:04:24,420 --> 00:04:26,780
Take a second to read through this,

61
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:30,460
noting the different categories that we're assessing,

62
00:04:30,760 --> 00:04:34,380
as well as the pertinent negatives for each of these categories,

63
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:38,120
including a mention of the quality of the examination.

64
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:42,660
Before we get into the details of the search pattern,

65
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:44,580
I just want to take a step back

66
00:04:44,580 --> 00:04:47,960
and really focus on the five key features

67
00:04:47,960 --> 00:04:51,040
that we need to assess on each and every head CT.

68
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:55,520
We need to evaluate the ventricles and extra-axial spaces.

69
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:58,760
This will give us an overall idea of brain volume.

70
00:04:59,680 --> 00:05:03,080
We really need to scrutinize for acute intracranial hemorrhage.

71
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:05,040
This is obviously something we don't want to miss.

72
00:05:05,960 --> 00:05:07,840
You're going to look for masses directly,

73
00:05:08,120 --> 00:05:09,700
as well as mass effect,

74
00:05:10,480 --> 00:05:12,640
including midline displacement of structures.

75
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:16,480
We're going to specifically look at the gray-white matter differentiation,

76
00:05:17,040 --> 00:05:18,260
which should be maintained.

77
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:20,960
Loss of this is going to suggest ischemia.

78
00:05:21,960 --> 00:05:26,060
And then finally, we want to always assess the basal cisterns.

79
00:05:26,420 --> 00:05:28,500
What are their contents, and are they patent?

80
00:05:31,050 --> 00:05:34,110
Now let's begin to look into the details of this search pattern.

81
00:05:34,590 --> 00:05:36,330
We're going to begin with the ventricles,

82
00:05:36,970 --> 00:05:38,610
assessing three key features.

83
00:05:39,570 --> 00:05:41,770
First, let's look at the morphology.

84
00:05:42,330 --> 00:05:43,810
What are the shape of the ventricles?

85
00:05:43,810 --> 00:05:46,690
And in the case of the lateral ventricles, are they symmetric?

86
00:05:48,270 --> 00:05:50,930
The overall size of the ventricles will be important.

87
00:05:51,210 --> 00:05:52,890
Are they too big or too small?

88
00:05:53,530 --> 00:05:57,110
And then finally, the attenuation of the contents of the ventricle,

89
00:05:57,510 --> 00:05:59,950
which, remember, should be simple fluid attenuation

90
00:05:59,950 --> 00:06:03,170
because ventricles contain cerebrospinal fluid.

91
00:06:03,490 --> 00:06:07,630
So we want to make sure that there's no high attenuation hemorrhage within the ventricles.

92
00:06:07,630 --> 00:06:12,790
Let's look at some examples of abnormal ventricular morphology.

93
00:06:13,550 --> 00:06:17,530
The key at this point is to be cognizant of the fact that you should be assessing

94
00:06:17,530 --> 00:06:22,270
for ventricular morphology in symmetry when interpreting a head CT.

95
00:06:23,090 --> 00:06:28,970
At this point, you are not going to be able to characterize any or all of these ventricular abnormalities.

96
00:06:28,970 --> 00:06:33,690
Just be aware of the fact that you need to be looking for morphology in symmetry.

97
00:06:35,110 --> 00:06:38,990
The normal lateral ventricles have a banana or bowtie shape,

98
00:06:39,250 --> 00:06:45,570
as illustrated on the right-sided image, which is an axial T2-weighted sequence.

99
00:06:46,830 --> 00:06:49,090
Notice the curvilinear or banana shape.

100
00:06:49,090 --> 00:06:53,870
Contrast that with the abnormal morphology of the lateral ventricles.

101
00:06:54,510 --> 00:06:58,050
In the case on the left, which is an axial CT image,

102
00:06:59,430 --> 00:07:03,410
notice the parallel configuration of the lateral ventricles

103
00:07:03,410 --> 00:07:11,030
in addition to dilatation of the ventricular trigone or ventricular atrium.

104
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:16,400
This is a case of agenesis of the corpus callosum,

105
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:20,580
resulting in abnormal ventricular morphology.

106
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:24,680
Another case of abnormal morphology.

107
00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:27,640
Notice on the normal case on the right,

108
00:07:28,160 --> 00:07:31,560
between the lateral ventricles, we're going to have the septum pellucidum,

109
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:32,620
a normal structure.

110
00:07:33,360 --> 00:07:37,620
Notice on the case on the left, in this patient with septooptic dysplasia,

111
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:40,560
they have an absent septum pellucidum.

112
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,300
So again, at this stage, the important part is not to know the details,

113
00:07:44,420 --> 00:07:47,280
but just to be aware of the fact that you should be looking

114
00:07:47,280 --> 00:07:49,900
at the underlying morphology of the ventricles.

115
00:07:49,900 --> 00:07:53,920
Apart from congenital abnormalities,

116
00:07:54,240 --> 00:08:01,400
sometimes subtle asymmetry of the ventricles may be a sign of underlying mass from mass effect.

117
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:05,080
This is an example of a not-so-subtle case

118
00:08:05,080 --> 00:08:09,520
where we have the left lateral ventricle being pushed in or effaced,

119
00:08:09,900 --> 00:08:16,100
secondary to mass effect from this area of vasogenic edema from underlying metastasis.

120
00:08:16,100 --> 00:08:20,280
And we're going to talk about the concept of vasogenic edema a little later on.

121
00:08:20,280 --> 00:08:27,800
The key here is asymmetric appearance of the ventricles as an indicator of an underlying mass.

122
00:08:30,320 --> 00:08:32,480
The ventricles contain 3-brospinal fluid,

123
00:08:32,940 --> 00:08:36,320
so their content should measure simple fluid attenuation.

124
00:08:36,540 --> 00:08:40,760
So we want to make sure when we're assessing the ventricles that they contain fluid.

125
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:43,860
They're also going to contain the corroid plexus,

126
00:08:43,860 --> 00:08:46,340
as we'll talk about a little later on, which can be a fuller,

127
00:08:46,340 --> 00:08:50,360
or it can be a high attenuation focus within the ventricles.

128
00:08:50,700 --> 00:08:55,240
But in general, we want to make sure that there's no blood or mass within the ventricles.

129
00:08:55,900 --> 00:08:59,700
Here's an example of high attenuation blood replacing the CSF.

130
00:09:00,140 --> 00:09:02,280
This is a case of intraventricular hemorrhage.

131
00:09:02,860 --> 00:09:06,920
This is caused from an underlying intraparenchymal hematoma

132
00:09:06,920 --> 00:09:09,620
that extended then into the ventricle.

133
00:09:09,620 --> 00:09:13,360
Assessment of ventricular size will be important.

134
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:15,700
Are the ventricles too large or too small?

135
00:09:16,440 --> 00:09:20,200
Small ventricles may be a sequelae of a congenital abnormality,

136
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:23,740
or as in the case shown here, over shunting.

137
00:09:24,540 --> 00:09:27,340
So in the case illustrated on this slide,

138
00:09:27,860 --> 00:09:31,420
we have successive images from the same patient at a similar level

139
00:09:31,420 --> 00:09:37,120
through the lateral ventricles in 2014, 2015, and 2016.

140
00:09:37,120 --> 00:09:41,620
If we look at the size of the lateral ventricles specifically here,

141
00:09:42,380 --> 00:09:46,680
we can see that with each year, the ventricles get progressively smaller.

142
00:09:47,240 --> 00:09:52,580
And this is a case of over shunting as a cause of the decreased ventricular size,

143
00:09:52,700 --> 00:09:54,580
an important finding to make in this patient.

144
00:09:55,980 --> 00:10:00,560
On the other end of the spectrum, we have dilated ventricles or ventriculomegaly.

145
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:06,780
In practice, you are typically going to be trying to distinguish between hydrocephalus

146
00:10:06,780 --> 00:10:12,600
and age-related cerebral atrophy resulting in enlarged ventricles.

147
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:17,860
This can often be very difficult and is a matter of experience and practice.

148
00:10:20,310 --> 00:10:25,150
One principle which is important to remember is that brain volume will depend on the age.

149
00:10:25,670 --> 00:10:29,530
You will have increasing atrophy of the brain parenchyma with increasing age.

150
00:10:29,530 --> 00:10:33,550
So as the brain atrophies, the ventricles become more dilated.

151
00:10:33,930 --> 00:10:40,050
As we can see in this example, comparing the brain of a 17-year-old with that of a 77-year-old.

152
00:10:40,450 --> 00:10:46,350
Notice in the case of the 77-year-old, there is enlargement of the ventricles and sulci relative

153
00:10:46,350 --> 00:10:51,810
to the 17-year-old secondary to atrophy of the brain parenchyma.

154
00:10:51,810 --> 00:10:57,350
Now, the question will become clinically, are the ventricles dilated out of proportion to

155
00:10:57,350 --> 00:10:59,430
the patient's age or perceived brain volume?

156
00:10:59,830 --> 00:11:05,090
This will be a hard distinction for you to make early on, but you just need to be aware

157
00:11:05,090 --> 00:11:11,590
of the fact that you need to be assessing for overall brain volume as you evaluate a

158
00:11:11,590 --> 00:11:12,070
head CT.

159
00:11:12,070 --> 00:11:17,980
This is another example of age-related cerebral atrophy.

160
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:23,980
So with age, the parenchymal volume decreases, which results in relative enlargement of the

161
00:11:23,980 --> 00:11:30,560
sulci and ventricles, as shown in this case, where we have relative prominence of the frontal

162
00:11:30,560 --> 00:11:36,860
horn of the lateral ventricle, the temporal horn of the lateral ventricle, the fourth ventricle,

163
00:11:36,860 --> 00:11:39,060
as well as the sylvian fissures.

164
00:11:39,060 --> 00:11:44,660
Don't forget to assess the cerebellar volume as well.

165
00:11:45,300 --> 00:11:48,640
You can also see age-related atrophy of the cerebellum.

166
00:11:49,140 --> 00:11:55,220
However, if the degree of atrophy appears advanced for age, you need to look into other causes of

167
00:11:55,220 --> 00:12:01,800
atrophy, such as chronic alcohol use or chronic use of the seizure medication phenytoin.

168
00:12:01,980 --> 00:12:09,100
In this example, we see advanced cerebellar atrophy for age, resulting in enlargement of the

169
00:12:09,100 --> 00:12:16,060
fissures of the cerebellum as identified on the axial view, coronal view, and sagittal view.

170
00:12:19,150 --> 00:12:25,590
So when assessing potential ventricular enlargement on head CT, you need to ask yourself this question.

171
00:12:26,290 --> 00:12:30,810
Are the ventricles dilated out of proportion to the degree of volume loss?

172
00:12:31,190 --> 00:12:36,430
If the answer to this is yes, then you need to start thinking about hydrocephalus.

173
00:12:36,430 --> 00:12:44,250
So hydrocephalus literally means water on the brain and represents the abnormal collection

174
00:12:44,250 --> 00:12:48,990
of cerebrospinal fluid within the brain, resulting in increased intracranial pressure.

175
00:12:49,670 --> 00:12:52,070
This comes in two basic forms.

176
00:12:52,730 --> 00:12:59,070
The first is termed non-communicating or intraventricular obstructive hydrocephalus, IVOH.

177
00:12:59,070 --> 00:13:05,970
And the second form is termed communicating or extraventricular obstructive hydrocephalus.

178
00:13:08,600 --> 00:13:14,980
So I think it's best to think about all forms of hydrocephalus as being obstructive, as was

179
00:13:14,980 --> 00:13:16,360
implied on the prior slide.

180
00:13:17,140 --> 00:13:20,940
The classification is actually going to depend on the level of obstruction.

181
00:13:20,940 --> 00:13:28,240
So with non-communicating or intraventricular obstructive hydrocephalus, the obstruction

182
00:13:28,240 --> 00:13:32,880
is going to be within the ventricles or at the ventricular outflow levels.

183
00:13:33,460 --> 00:13:38,960
So this is termed non-communicating relative to the ventricles in the sense that if there's

184
00:13:38,960 --> 00:13:45,560
a blockage within the ventricles, that the ventricles don't communicate with one another.

185
00:13:45,560 --> 00:13:53,960
Now, communicating hydrocephalus or extraventricular obstructive hydrocephalus means that there is

186
00:13:53,960 --> 00:14:01,080
communication within the parts of the ventricular system, but the obstruction is present, but

187
00:14:01,080 --> 00:14:07,260
it happens to be distal to the fourth ventricular outlet foramen, which are the foramen of Magendi

188
00:14:07,260 --> 00:14:07,840
and Lushka.

189
00:14:10,530 --> 00:14:16,770
So in summary, think of all hydrocephalus as obstruction with the category of obstruction depending

190
00:14:16,770 --> 00:14:18,790
on the level of the abnormality.

191
00:14:19,390 --> 00:14:23,510
Is it within the ventricles or is it distal to the fourth ventricular outlet foramen?

192
00:14:26,320 --> 00:14:30,920
To understand the image and appearance of the different types of hydrocephalus, we need

193
00:14:30,920 --> 00:14:37,960
to understand the CSF circulation and the principle that ventricular dilatation will be proximal

194
00:14:37,960 --> 00:14:42,680
to the level of obstruction based on this CSF flow pattern.

195
00:14:42,680 --> 00:14:48,560
So we're going to have the cerebrospinal fluid, which is produced in the ventricles by the

196
00:14:48,560 --> 00:14:49,400
coroid plexus.

197
00:14:49,540 --> 00:14:57,780
So we're going to follow the CSF as it starts out in the lateral ventricle, flows through

198
00:14:57,780 --> 00:15:06,000
the foramen of Monroe into the third ventricle, through the cerebral aqueduct into the fourth

199
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:13,240
ventricle, and then it will flow out through the fourth ventricular outlet foramen, medially

200
00:15:13,240 --> 00:15:17,020
which will be Magendi, and laterally which will be the foramen of Lushka.

201
00:15:18,840 --> 00:15:25,060
The CSF will then circulate around the cord, around the cerebral convexities, and eventually

202
00:15:25,060 --> 00:15:27,420
be resorbed by the arachnoid granulations.

203
00:15:27,420 --> 00:15:36,560
The boundary between the level of obstruction that's considered interventricular or non-communicating

204
00:15:36,560 --> 00:15:41,560
hydrocephalus, and that which is considered communicating or extraventricular obstructive

205
00:15:41,560 --> 00:15:46,920
hydrocephalus, is the fourth ventricular outlet foramen, as shown here.

206
00:15:48,240 --> 00:15:54,560
Once the CSF moves beyond these outlet foramen, it will circulate around the spinal cord and

207
00:15:54,560 --> 00:16:01,160
brain along the cerebral convexities, and eventually be resorbed by the arachnoid granulations.

208
00:16:02,020 --> 00:16:07,320
So obstruction at the level of the arachnoid villi, such as might occur with subarachnoid

209
00:16:07,320 --> 00:16:13,740
hemorrhage, where you have blood plugging up the arachnoid granulations, this would be considered

210
00:16:13,740 --> 00:16:18,320
communicating or extraventricular obstructive hydrocephalus.

211
00:16:18,680 --> 00:16:25,860
Now let's see how these principles apply to clinical practice and evaluation of the

212
00:16:25,860 --> 00:16:27,080
ventricular enlargement.

213
00:16:27,520 --> 00:16:32,280
Here we have ventricles which are dilated out of proportion to the degree of volume loss.

214
00:16:32,700 --> 00:16:35,420
The question is, what ventricles are dilated?

215
00:16:35,940 --> 00:16:39,860
So we're going to have enlargement of the lateral ventricles, as we see here.

216
00:16:40,540 --> 00:16:45,740
The third ventricle is quite large, but if you'll notice the fourth ventricle, that's normal.

217
00:16:46,160 --> 00:16:50,520
So let's think about the pattern and the flow of CSF with the principle that we're going

218
00:16:50,520 --> 00:16:55,020
to get ventricular enlargement proximal to the level of obstruction.

219
00:16:55,380 --> 00:16:57,800
Where do you think the level of obstruction is here?

220
00:16:58,680 --> 00:17:03,540
Again, we have dilated third and lateral ventricles with a normal fourth ventricle.

221
00:17:03,740 --> 00:17:10,620
So this would be an example of non-communicating or intraventricular obstructive hydrocephalus.

222
00:17:10,620 --> 00:17:15,960
That is, the obstruction is within the ventricles itself, so that the ventricles are not communicating

223
00:17:15,960 --> 00:17:16,680
with each other.

224
00:17:17,160 --> 00:17:22,680
And based on this pattern, the level of obstruction should be at the level of the cerebral aqueduct,

225
00:17:23,240 --> 00:17:30,040
therefore giving you dilatation proximal to this with respect to CSF flow within the

226
00:17:30,040 --> 00:17:35,000
lateral and third ventricles, but a normal fourth ventricle which is distal to the obstruction.

227
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:42,220
This is another example of ventricular enlargement out of proportion to the degree of parenchymal

228
00:17:42,220 --> 00:17:42,960
volume loss.

229
00:17:43,740 --> 00:17:46,360
What is the pattern of ventricular enlargement?

230
00:17:46,780 --> 00:17:49,080
We're going to have dilated lateral ventricles.

231
00:17:49,480 --> 00:17:54,820
Here we see the body of the lateral ventricle dilated, and even more importantly, as it is

232
00:17:54,820 --> 00:18:01,660
often the best indicator of dilatation of the lateral ventricles, you can see here the temporal

233
00:18:01,660 --> 00:18:04,760
horn of the lateral ventricle is dilated.

234
00:18:05,820 --> 00:18:10,240
In this case, the third ventricle and the fourth ventricle are also dilated.

235
00:18:11,300 --> 00:18:17,180
So our pattern is diffuse enlargement of the intraventricular system, which would be an example

236
00:18:17,180 --> 00:18:22,320
of communicating or extraventricular obstructive hydrocephalus.

237
00:18:23,060 --> 00:18:27,880
Here the level of obstruction was assumed to be at the arachnoid villi.

238
00:18:27,880 --> 00:18:40,140
The key to correctly answering this question is the ability to identify the pattern of ventricular

239
00:18:40,140 --> 00:18:40,740
enlargement.

240
00:18:41,200 --> 00:18:47,500
In this case, as is typical, the assessment of the lateral ventricles is best done on the

241
00:18:47,500 --> 00:18:48,320
temporal horns.

242
00:18:48,580 --> 00:18:51,700
Here we can see the temporal horns of the lateral ventricles are dilated.

243
00:18:52,440 --> 00:18:54,020
The frontal horns are also dilated.

244
00:18:54,740 --> 00:18:59,960
However, the third ventricle is shown here, and the fourth ventricle is shown here, are

245
00:18:59,960 --> 00:19:00,960
normal in caliber.

246
00:19:00,960 --> 00:19:09,100
However, so given this pattern and our knowledge of CSF flow dynamics, we know the level of obstruction

247
00:19:09,100 --> 00:19:11,220
has to be within the ventricular system.

248
00:19:11,780 --> 00:19:14,840
Specifically, this is at the level of the foramen of Monroe.

249
00:19:15,520 --> 00:19:20,360
And if we look closely at this area, we can actually see a mass, which is a colloid cyst,

250
00:19:20,360 --> 00:19:27,360
at the foramen of Monroe, obstructing the flow of CSF, resulting in proximal ventricular dilatation

251
00:19:27,360 --> 00:19:32,020
and the distal ventricles being decompressed or normal in caliber.

252
00:19:32,460 --> 00:19:39,660
Now that we've assessed the ventricles and sulci and have gotten an idea of the brain volume

253
00:19:39,660 --> 00:19:44,600
and whether or not hydrocephalus is present, we want to turn our attention to the detection

254
00:19:44,600 --> 00:19:47,060
of acute intracranial hemorrhage.

255
00:19:47,060 --> 00:19:52,480
To evaluate for acute hemorrhage will be a common indication for ordering a non-contrast

256
00:19:52,480 --> 00:19:53,500
head CT.

257
00:19:54,700 --> 00:20:01,360
The key is the high attenuation of the acute hemorrhage, typically ranging on the order

258
00:20:01,360 --> 00:20:08,460
of 40 to 70 Hounsfield units, where typical simple fluid or water attenuation ranges from

259
00:20:08,460 --> 00:20:10,920
approximately 0 to 20 Hounsfield units.

260
00:20:10,920 --> 00:20:17,340
And as we see in this case, we have an intracerebral hematoma in the right frontal region.

261
00:20:17,680 --> 00:20:25,010
Before we get too far into the discussion of localizing hemorrhage, I think it will be

262
00:20:25,010 --> 00:20:36,310
worthwhile to discuss an imaging concept that is central to determining the etiology of intracranial

263
00:20:36,310 --> 00:20:36,750
masses.

264
00:20:36,750 --> 00:20:43,150
That is, when we see an abnormal collection or mass within the cranium, we need to categorize

265
00:20:43,150 --> 00:20:50,070
it as either being intraaxial, implying it's within the brain parenchyma itself, versus

266
00:20:50,070 --> 00:20:54,950
extraaxial, which is actually external to the brain substance.

267
00:20:55,330 --> 00:20:59,870
This will be very helpful in providing a differential diagnosis for intracranial masses.

268
00:21:02,400 --> 00:21:05,520
Intraaxial masses are within the brain parenchyma.

269
00:21:05,520 --> 00:21:10,460
Here we have three different examples of intraaxial abnormalities.

270
00:21:11,060 --> 00:21:18,640
On the CT image on our left, we have a large intraaxial or intraparenchymal hematoma.

271
00:21:18,940 --> 00:21:24,460
Again, that high attenuation of acute hemorrhage on non-contrast head CT is evident.

272
00:21:25,900 --> 00:21:33,020
The case in the middle is an axial T2 flare image showing signal abnormality on the right side,

273
00:21:33,020 --> 00:21:34,620
secondary to a metastasis.

274
00:21:36,060 --> 00:21:42,960
And then finally, we have a T1-weighted image on the left showing an intraaxial signal abnormality,

275
00:21:43,520 --> 00:21:48,520
secondary to ischemia in the MCA territory.

276
00:21:49,860 --> 00:21:56,560
All of these abnormalities are within the brain substance and would be categorized as intraaxial.

277
00:21:56,560 --> 00:22:04,160
Extraaxial masses, on the other hand, are actually external to the brain substance itself.

278
00:22:05,360 --> 00:22:13,320
This is a case of a meningioma, a tumor arising from the meninges, which are along the surface

279
00:22:13,320 --> 00:22:13,940
of the brain.

280
00:22:14,440 --> 00:22:20,360
In this case, the mass is outside the brain substance, but pushing into the substance of

281
00:22:20,360 --> 00:22:22,120
the brain resulting in mass effect.

282
00:22:23,580 --> 00:22:28,420
This is another example of an extraaxial mass, again a meningioma.

283
00:22:29,460 --> 00:22:35,140
The key here is to recognize that the mass is actually external to the brain substance,

284
00:22:35,140 --> 00:22:40,920
although it is pushing in on the temporal lobe, exerting mass effect on the brain.

285
00:22:40,920 --> 00:22:47,720
Notice that the gray matter ribbon, as shown in the axial T2-weighted sequence on the left,

286
00:22:48,080 --> 00:22:54,420
is actually displaced inward by the mass with an intervening cleft, a cerebrospinal fluid.

287
00:22:55,140 --> 00:23:01,360
We see that same finding on the coronal T2-weighted image on the right.

288
00:23:02,360 --> 00:23:07,420
Again, that displacement of the gray matter ribbon with intervening CSF.

289
00:23:07,420 --> 00:23:17,110
Let's take a close-up view of the axial T2-weighted image in this case to really highlight the critical

290
00:23:17,110 --> 00:23:23,390
findings to definitively categorize this mass as being extraaxial in origin.

291
00:23:23,830 --> 00:23:29,570
We have the mass, as indicated by the asterisk, located in the anterior aspect of the left

292
00:23:29,570 --> 00:23:32,650
middle cranial fossa, pushing in on the temporal lobe.

293
00:23:33,490 --> 00:23:36,870
We're going to have inward displacement of that gray matter ribbon.

294
00:23:36,870 --> 00:23:44,770
And finally, we see that cleft of cerebrospinal fluid insinuating between the mass itself

295
00:23:44,770 --> 00:23:47,110
and the underlying gray matter.

296
00:23:48,070 --> 00:23:52,630
These are definitive signs for the mass being extraaxial in origin.

297
00:23:59,520 --> 00:24:01,420
So this is a little bit of a trick question.

298
00:24:02,080 --> 00:24:06,420
On the provided image, the mass certainly looks like it's within the brain substance.

299
00:24:06,420 --> 00:24:13,900
However, if you look on the coronal view, as shown on the left, we can see that the mass

300
00:24:13,900 --> 00:24:20,120
has a broad base of attachment to the floor of the middle cranial fossa and is actually

301
00:24:20,120 --> 00:24:21,280
extraaxial.

302
00:24:21,700 --> 00:24:27,640
However, this mimics an intraaxial mass on the axial image shown in the question and also

303
00:24:27,640 --> 00:24:29,820
shown on this slide on the right.

304
00:24:29,820 --> 00:24:34,760
The key here is simply to be aware of this pitfall and that you really need to view a

305
00:24:34,760 --> 00:24:40,320
mass in different planes when you're trying to categorize it as being intraaxial or extraaxial.

306
00:24:41,920 --> 00:24:47,820
Now that we have a firm grasp of the concept of intraaxial and extraaxial, let's turn our

307
00:24:47,820 --> 00:24:52,360
attention back specifically to localizing acute intracranial hemorrhage.

308
00:24:52,360 --> 00:24:59,040
As in this example, and as we have previously discussed, acute intracranial hemorrhage will

309
00:24:59,040 --> 00:25:00,240
be high in attenuation.

310
00:25:00,700 --> 00:25:05,840
In this example, we have a round high attenuation mass consistent with blood.

311
00:25:06,760 --> 00:25:10,460
Notice its location is intraaxial within the brain parenchyma.

312
00:25:11,260 --> 00:25:17,160
And also note there is mild edema within the adjacent white matter, as you can see by the

313
00:25:17,160 --> 00:25:22,640
slight decreased attenuation of the white matter adjacent to the mass.

314
00:25:24,920 --> 00:25:28,000
Extraaxial hemorrhage can come in a variety of forms.

315
00:25:28,880 --> 00:25:35,760
In the example on the left, we're going to have a collection of blood superficial to the dura.

316
00:25:36,260 --> 00:25:37,860
This is an epidural hematoma.

317
00:25:39,320 --> 00:25:42,580
Here we have a collection that is subdural in location.

318
00:25:42,580 --> 00:25:51,210
Notice this collection of blood manifests itself as high attenuation within one of the sulci

319
00:25:51,210 --> 00:25:55,430
of the cerebral hemisphere, in keeping with subarachnoid hemorrhage.

320
00:25:56,330 --> 00:26:02,550
And then finally, we have an example of high attenuation within the ventricular system, representing

321
00:26:02,550 --> 00:26:05,410
intraventricular hematoma.

322
00:26:07,520 --> 00:26:13,180
While typically it is straightforward to localize subarachnoid and intraventricular hemorrhage,

323
00:26:13,180 --> 00:26:19,020
distinguishing between epidural and subdural hematomas may sometimes be difficult.

324
00:26:19,740 --> 00:26:25,700
In this example, we're going to look at a coronal schematic view of the brain to try

325
00:26:25,700 --> 00:26:30,740
to highlight the differences in appearance and location of subdural and epidural hematomas.

326
00:26:31,320 --> 00:26:36,120
We're going to begin by looking at the dura itself, which is made up of two layers.

327
00:26:36,120 --> 00:26:47,880
You're going to have the superficial dural layer outlined in dark gray with the inner dura indicated

328
00:26:47,880 --> 00:26:48,780
by light gray.

329
00:26:49,580 --> 00:26:56,680
Notice that the layers of the inner dura split at the midline to create the dural venous sinuses,

330
00:26:56,680 --> 00:27:01,280
as indicated by the asterisks, in this case, the superior sagittal sinus.

331
00:27:01,280 --> 00:27:14,440
Epidural hematomas will be collections superficial to the outer layer of the dura, as indicated

332
00:27:14,440 --> 00:27:24,000
by the arrow, while subdural hematomas will collect deep to the inner layer of the dura, as indicated

333
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:37,120
here. Understanding this normal dural anatomy will be key to appreciation of the imaging features

334
00:27:37,120 --> 00:27:41,720
of subdural and epidural hematomas that we'll discuss in the next few slides.

335
00:27:43,500 --> 00:27:49,180
Subdural hematomas, as shown here, follow the inner layer of the dura.

336
00:27:49,800 --> 00:27:55,160
Given the dural anatomy, we can see that this collection will actually round the bend, so to speak,

337
00:27:55,160 --> 00:28:02,920
to follow the falx or the tentorium, and will not cross the midline, as in the coronal schematic

338
00:28:02,920 --> 00:28:08,720
we saw in the prior slide, and also will not cross from super-tentorial to infra-tentorial,

339
00:28:09,920 --> 00:28:16,820
given this same concept in dural anatomy. It often has a chryscentic shape on axial view,

340
00:28:17,320 --> 00:28:23,400
as indicated in this example. It is not affected by the sutures of the skull, as we'll discuss,

341
00:28:23,400 --> 00:28:30,100
this is a characteristic of an epidural hematoma, and the source of the bleed is typically a cortical

342
00:28:30,100 --> 00:28:38,380
vein, so these are typically venous in origin. Epidural hematomas form superficial to this outer

343
00:28:38,380 --> 00:28:46,160
layer of the dura, and as such can cross the midline along the falx, or can cross between the

344
00:28:46,160 --> 00:28:52,380
super-tentorial and infra-tentorial compartments along the tentorium. They are not limited by this

345
00:28:52,380 --> 00:28:59,360
split in the inner layer of the dura, as are subdural hematomas. However, they may be limited

346
00:28:59,360 --> 00:29:07,280
by sutures, as the dura is tightly adherent to the sutures. On axial images, epidurals are often

347
00:29:07,280 --> 00:29:16,600
different lentiform in shape, as we can see in this example. Unlike subdural hematomas, which are

348
00:29:16,600 --> 00:29:24,200
typically venous in origin, epidural hematomas are classically associated with arterial bleeding,

349
00:29:25,080 --> 00:29:31,640
often because of an underlying skull fracture. So, on the soft tissue or brain parenchymal windows

350
00:29:31,640 --> 00:29:38,280
on the left, we can see a left frontal epidural hematoma with its characteristic lentiform shape.

351
00:29:38,280 --> 00:29:45,540
If we use bone windows to help us get an idea of the underlying calvarium, we can see that this is

352
00:29:45,540 --> 00:29:53,340
associated with a minimally displaced calvarial fracture. Subarachnoid hemorrhage is typically

353
00:29:53,340 --> 00:29:58,280
pretty straightforward, at least in concept. It can sometimes be subtle and be hard to detect,

354
00:29:59,080 --> 00:30:07,060
but in this space, the hemorrhage will follow the CSF spaces. So, on our example on the left,

355
00:30:07,060 --> 00:30:13,660
we have a subtle area of high attenuation within a sulcus. This is subtle subarachnoid

356
00:30:13,660 --> 00:30:20,240
hemorrhage. The case on the right is not so subtle. Remember that the basal cisterns are these

357
00:30:20,240 --> 00:30:26,720
reservoirs for CSF at the skull base, and we can see extensive subarachnoid hemorrhage replacing a

358
00:30:26,720 --> 00:30:36,060
normal fluid attenuation of the basal cisterns. A potential pitfall in assessing for acute

359
00:30:36,060 --> 00:30:42,440
intracranial hemorrhage on head CT is the presence of calcifications, which can also be high attenuation.

360
00:30:43,200 --> 00:30:48,360
Here are three examples of commonly encountered calcifications that you will see on a head CT.

361
00:30:49,160 --> 00:30:55,760
On the case on the left, we have calcification within the basal ganglia. As evident in this case,

362
00:30:56,160 --> 00:31:02,780
symmetric involvement is often a clue that this represents calcification and not hemorrhage.

363
00:31:02,780 --> 00:31:11,140
In the middle case, we identify calcification of the coroid plexus. And then finally, on the case

364
00:31:11,140 --> 00:31:18,960
on the right, we see calcification within the pineal gland. So, when you identify subtle high

365
00:31:18,960 --> 00:31:25,620
attenuation within, or when you're evaluating a head CT, always keep in the back of your mind,

366
00:31:25,720 --> 00:31:38,200
could this potentially be calcification? The first thing we want to notice about this collection

367
00:31:38,200 --> 00:31:44,400
is that it is displacing the gray matter inward. So, this would be expected to be superficial to the

368
00:31:44,400 --> 00:31:50,580
brain substance itself, indicative of an extra-axial fluid collection. On axial images,

369
00:31:50,880 --> 00:31:57,740
the chrysanthic shape is characteristic of a subdural hematoma. It is also fairly common for

370
00:31:57,740 --> 00:32:04,380
subdural hematomas to spread out over a fairly large area within the hemisphere. On the other hand,

371
00:32:04,380 --> 00:32:10,620
epidural hematomas will typically assume a lenticular shape on axial images, while the subarachnoid hemorrhage

372
00:32:10,620 --> 00:32:16,920
will follow the sulci and be located within the basal cisterns, again, the CSF spaces.

373
00:32:17,380 --> 00:32:25,820
So far, we've discussed the identification of acute intracranial hemorrhage by its characteristic

374
00:32:25,820 --> 00:32:32,660
high attenuation. However, we need to be cognizant of the fact that this attenuation will vary

375
00:32:32,660 --> 00:32:40,200
depending on the time between the bleed and when the patient is imaged. So, in this example,

376
00:32:40,620 --> 00:32:46,540
we have a hyperdense or acute subdural hematoma as shown by the arrow on the left-sided image.

377
00:32:46,980 --> 00:32:51,600
This is the characteristic appearance of acute intracranial hemorrhage as we've discussed.

378
00:32:52,380 --> 00:32:58,280
However, as you'll notice with time, these are different patients in different locations of the

379
00:32:58,280 --> 00:33:05,860
bleed. We can see that the attenuation of the hematoma will decrease. So, for example, with a

380
00:33:05,860 --> 00:33:13,200
chronic hematoma as imaged on the right side, the right-handed image, we have a hypodense

381
00:33:13,200 --> 00:33:21,220
collection along the right frontal lobe. Notice that as the hematoma goes from hyperdense relative

382
00:33:21,220 --> 00:33:27,460
to the brain parenchyma to hypodense, there will be a phase in between, usually in the subacute

383
00:33:27,460 --> 00:33:34,820
phase, where that hematoma will be isodense to the underlying gray matter and will be difficult to

384
00:33:34,820 --> 00:33:41,720
detect, as we can see in the middle image where there's a subtle left frontal hematoma. Isodense

385
00:33:41,720 --> 00:33:49,240
or isoattenuating to the underlying gray matter. Let's take a closer look of the case where we have

386
00:33:49,240 --> 00:33:55,980
an isodense subdural. That is, when the subdural hematoma is isoattenuating or the same attenuation

387
00:33:55,980 --> 00:34:01,780
as the underlying gray matter, such that it blends in with the gray matter and is difficult to identify.

388
00:34:02,420 --> 00:34:10,980
What are some of the clues that we can use to identify the presence of this hematoma? Well, this is

389
00:34:10,980 --> 00:34:18,340
the same example where we see a subtle isodense left subdural hematoma. One of the things that we can

390
00:34:18,340 --> 00:34:24,640
look for is actually buckling of the underlying white matter. So if you compare the white matter

391
00:34:24,640 --> 00:34:29,940
in terms of symmetry between right and left, you can see in this example how the left-sided

392
00:34:29,940 --> 00:34:35,060
white matter is buckled in from the mass effect from the adjacent subdural hematoma.

393
00:34:35,940 --> 00:34:43,560
In older patients, this may manifest itself as a lack of expected sulci, or asymmetric appearance of

394
00:34:43,560 --> 00:34:49,060
the sulci. These can be very hard to detect, though, if they are bilateral and symmetric.

395
00:34:51,750 --> 00:34:57,570
In this module, we began our discussion of a systematic search pattern for interpreting a head CT.

396
00:34:58,470 --> 00:35:03,590
We covered assessment of the ventricles and extra-axial spaces, as well as the evaluation

397
00:35:03,590 --> 00:35:09,150
for acute intracranial hemorrhage. One thing we need to keep in the back of our mind as we

398
00:35:09,150 --> 00:35:14,210
go through our search pattern is that we may need to use different windows to highlight the tissue

399
00:35:14,210 --> 00:35:22,050
that we're specifically evaluating for in that portion of the search pattern. In part two, we'll

400
00:35:22,050 --> 00:35:28,450
complete our discussion of the search pattern after we discuss parenchymal evaluation, as well as

401
00:35:28,450 --> 00:35:36,260
evaluation of the bones and surrounding soft tissues. To summarize the basic imaging concepts that we've

402
00:35:36,260 --> 00:35:42,660
learned about in this module, we began with discussion of the ventricles, and particularly the ventricular

403
00:35:42,660 --> 00:35:49,140
size. Are the ventricles too large or too small? In the case of large ventricles, we addressed the key

404
00:35:49,140 --> 00:35:55,140
question that you need to think about in interpretation. That is, are the ventricles dilated out of proportion

405
00:35:55,140 --> 00:36:05,080
to the degree of parenchymal volume loss? That is, are we dealing with age-related atrophy or hydrocephalus?

406
00:36:05,080 --> 00:36:12,040
We then talked about the different types or categories of hydrocephalus and how to evaluate

407
00:36:12,040 --> 00:36:17,240
a head CT to distinguish which category of disease we're dealing with based on the pattern

408
00:36:17,240 --> 00:36:23,960
of ventricular enlargement. Then finally, we moved on to the detection of acute intracranial hemorrhage,

409
00:36:24,680 --> 00:36:32,200
talking about localization of fluid collections and masses as either intraaxial or within the substance

410
00:36:32,200 --> 00:36:41,320
of the brain versus extra-axial. We finally went into distinguishing the different types of extra-axial

411
00:36:42,440 --> 00:36:48,680
fluid collections with an emphasis on distinguishing subdural from epidural hematomas based on our

412
00:36:48,680 --> 00:36:56,360
knowledge of normal dural anatomy. And then we ended by our discussion of the expected evolution that

413
00:36:56,360 --> 00:37:06,450
we would have in terms of its attenuation of intracranial extra-axial hematomas. This brings us to the end of

414
00:37:06,450 --> 00:37:50,400
the unit. Please complete the corresponding quiz questions to receive credit for this course. If you would like

415
00:37:50,400 --> 00:37:55,920
to receive credit for this course, please click the log off button at the bottom of the screen.

